<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eppillus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 1st century BCE – early 1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eppillus was a British king of the late 1st century BCE and early 1st century CE known from his coins, which call him a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commius">Commius</a>. He appears to have ruled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calleva-atrebatum">Calleva Atrebatum</a> and later in Kent. Like his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tincomarus">Tincomarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verica">Verica</a>, he issued coins in Roman style with the title REX, suggesting friendly relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The name Eppillus is Celtic for little horse. He was a Roman client king of the Atrebates, a tribe of the British Iron Age, and ruled part of the territory held before him by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commius">Commius</a>, the Gaulish former ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> after the uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>, or possibly by a son of Commius. No historical writer mentions Eppillus, and he is known only from his coins. Their inscription COMMI.FILI is generally read as Commios filius, showing at least a claim to be the son of Commius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commius">Commius</a> in about 20 BCE, the coins suggest that Eppillus ruled jointly with another ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tincomarus">Tincomarus</a>, whose coins carry the same COMMI.FILI inscription, so that the two may have been brothers. A little before 7 CE Eppillus became ruler of the whole territory. Tincomarus appears as a supplicant to the Emperor Augustus in his Res Gestae and seems to have been driven out in some domestic intrigue. From then on the coins of Eppillus are marked Rex, a sign that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> recognised him as king.</p><p>A single stater found at Dover bears the names of Eppillus and an otherwise unknown Anarevito. It has been suggested that the two were allied rulers, but their relationship is unclear. In about 15 CE Verica succeeded Eppillus as king of the Atrebates. At about the same time coins of the Cantiaci stamped with the name Eppillus begin to appear in Kent, replacing those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubnovellaunus">Dubnovellaunus</a>.</p><p>Several explanations have been offered. Eppillus may have been deposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verica">Verica</a> and fled to Kent to make himself king there, or the Cantiaci may have invited him to rule them, and he handed the Atrebates peacefully to Verica. It is equally possible that he died, that Verica succeeded him, and that the Eppillus of Kent was another man of the same name. Verica's coins also bear the COMMI.FILI inscription, perhaps marking a third brother, although his possible presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 47 CE would have required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commius">Commius</a> to live a very long life.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eppillus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings claiming descent from Commius, notably Tincomarus, <b>Eppillus</b> and Verica, issued gold coins that increasingly copied Roman designs, and Verica's carry a vine leaf and the title REX, signs of friendship with Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates-britain">Atrebates (Britain)</a></li></ul></section>
